// "Draw N" ([CR#121.1]): the imperative YOU-form draw — you draw N cards, ONE
// AT A TIME ([CR#121.2] — a per-card SEQUENCE, NOT a simultaneous batch like
// mill). A `Batch(N, ...)` over the per-unit `By(You, DrawCard)`. The `Batch` gives
// a count-doubling replacement (Alhammarret's Archive, [CR#121.2a,616.1g]) ONE
// aggregate window; the engine then runs the N contained draws SEQUENTIALLY —
// each binds the library top LATE and empty-checks BEFORE its move, so an empty
// library loses the game ([CR#121.4,104.3c]).
//
// NOT a keyword action, and NOT a `Composite`: [CR#701] enumerates the keyword
// actions and drawing is not among them — it is [CR#121], a game action.
// Drawing is also irreducible, so there is no body to store: [CR#121.5] states
// that a library → Hand move made WITHOUT the word "draw" is not a draw, so
// such a body would denote a different event. The performer rides `By`'s own
// agent slot. (This macro keeps the `KeywordAction` macro kind: that kind is the
// English VERB REGISTRY — the conjugation data the renderer needs — not a CR
// [CR#701] claim. See `Mill.ron` for a body that IS its verb's meaning.)
//
// The declarative-subject form ("Target player draws N cards.") is the
// `Draws(who, n)` macro; this is the bare you-imperative.
//
// Usage: Draw(1) -> "Draw a card." / Draw(3) -> "Draw three cards."
(
    name: "Draw",
    template: "draw ${0:card|cards}",
    kinds: [OneShotEffect, KeywordAction],
    params: [Count],
    // The checked-in `template:` uses the `card|cards` pluralization codec,
    // which `cargo xtask macro templates --check` cannot express as a
    // projection of frame text (D10) — so this def is reported `excepted`,
    // not divergent, regardless of this frame's exact wording. "cards" is
    // picked over "card" arbitrarily; citation normalization (Task 4) makes
    // either authoring compile to the same tree.
    frames: ["draw <Param(0)> cards"],
    body: Batch(Param(0), By(You, DrawCard)),
)
